---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/abandoned-cart";
const title = "Shopify abandoned cart recovery: a send eligibility contract";
const desc = "Define recoverable cart events, identity, inventory, suppression, offers and measurement before choosing a cart recovery app.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should the first reminder send?",
    "answer": "Choose a delay from your buying cycle and the integration\u2019s observed event latency. Recheck purchase, permission, cart version and product state before dispatch."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every reminder include a discount?",
    "answer": "No. Resolve purchase friction first. If testing an offer, define its eligibility, stacking and contribution constraints and compare against a suitable alternative."
  },
  {
    "question": "What recovery rate should I expect?",
    "answer": "No rate is established here. Define the eligible cart population and order-linking rule, then measure your baseline. Attributed recovery alone does not show causal lift."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <p class="editorial-kicker">Shopify email operating library</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">A cart event is a candidate for follow-up, not permission to send. Build a recovery workflow that can explain which cart is current, whether the shopper is reachable, and why the message is still useful at dispatch. The rehearsal below is proposed configuration work, not a report of merchant results.</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Distinguish cart from checkout and browse events</h2>
      <p>Record the exact event your integration supplies: an item added to a cart, a checkout started, or a checkout left incomplete. Keep event ID, cart or checkout ID, customer identity, occurrence time and ingestion time. Do not label every product view an abandoned cart or assume every anonymous cart has a reachable address.</p><p>Define abandonment from the latest relevant activity and your observed buying cycle. Document whether a new event resets the timer or replaces the existing candidate. Browse follow-up is a separate workflow with different evidence of intent.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Establish identity and eligibility separately</h2>
      <p>Resolve the candidate to a contact only when the mapping is supported. Keep unknown identities out of sending instead of guessing from a similar email or device. An address appearing in checkout data does not alone settle the permission and policy conditions for marketing follow-up.</p><p>At dispatch, recheck the applicable permission, opt-out and suppression state for that channel. Compare guest and known-customer candidates separately so an identity coverage gap is visible. Do not pressure guests to create an account simply to improve a recovery report.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Treat the cart as changing state</h2>
      <p>Deduplicate repeated event deliveries by their stable identifier. Store a candidate version so an older event arriving late cannot overwrite a newer cart. Define expiry and re-entry rules that fit the catalog; a new shopping session should not revive an old coupon automatically.</p><p>Purchase, cart replacement and manual pause can invalidate a pending job. Re-evaluate eligibility near dispatch and cancel stale jobs when possible. Record the event-to-dispatch delay and the remaining race window; no integration should be described as having instantaneous cancellation without evidence.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Choose timing from customer friction</h2>
      <p>Use observed time-to-purchase and support questions to choose an initial delay and follow-up horizon. A considered furniture order may need dimensions or delivery information; a subscription starter may need cadence and cancellation details. Neither needs a universal three-email schedule.</p><p>Use an explicit channel priority and contact budget. An unrecorded email open is weak evidence for escalating to SMS. If adding a text, verify that channel’s eligibility and evaluate its additional value and cost independently.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Validate every product promise</h2>
      <p>Before composing the message, check the variant, current price, purchasability and destination. Decide what happens when only part of the cart remains available. Removing a stale line, pausing the message and offering a clearly labeled alternative are different choices; do not silently substitute an item in a checkout link.</p><p>Do not imply stock is reserved or an offer expires unless those conditions are implemented. For an unavailable variant, a <a href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">back-in-stock</a> invitation is a separate signup choice, not automatic enrollment.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Resolve welcome and campaign collisions</h2>
      <p>Create a contact-level decision table for welcome, cart, browse, winback and sale messages. A purchase can stop a cart reminder while allowing an appropriate service message. Specify which pending marketing job pauses, how long the condition lasts, and who owns restoring it.</p><p>Make the policy visible to every sender, including native automations and legacy apps. A paper priority rule is insufficient if the second sender never receives the suppression state. During migration, pause the incumbent’s equivalent workflow before the replacement sends.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Approve an offer before automating it</h2>
      <p>Start with the question the customer needs answered. If using an incentive, document product eligibility, stacking, redemption limit, expiry and a contribution floor approved for that offer. Check subscription and existing sale interactions. Previous coupon use alone does not prove that another discount is necessary.</p><p>Calculate order contribution from net merchandise revenue after discounts and refunds, less product cost, variable fulfillment, payment fees and the shipping subsidy you bear. Subtract messaging cost at the program level. Do not subtract a discount again if it is already in net revenue. See <a href="/guides/discount-discipline">discount discipline</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Rehearse failures without live customers</h2>
      <p>Proposed non-sending fixture set: duplicate event, late old cart, purchase during the delay, unsubscribe after enrollment, unavailable size, partially unavailable cart, changed price, failed product lookup and overlapping welcome offer. For each, record the expected action and actual preview or job state.</p><p>Also rehearse a broken return link and a delayed purchase sync. Fail closed for unresolved eligibility and product state according to the written policy. Keep a pause control and owner available; a vendor demonstration should show these fixtures rather than promise a recovery percentage.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Report the funnel with defined denominators</h2>
      <p>Count candidate carts, resolved identities, eligible contacts, dispatched messages, delivery outcomes, clicks and linked orders separately. Name whether recovery means an order linked to the original checkout, a later order by the customer, or a platform-attributed purchase. Do not divide one definition’s numerator by another’s denominator.</p><p>Break results down by guest status, cart value, offer and subscription context without double-counting carts that received several touches. Observed recovery is not incremental recovery. Use <a href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a> for reconciliation and <a href="/guides/revenue-attribution">attribution</a> for causal evaluation.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card mb-8"><h2>Use the contract to evaluate apps and hand off</h2>
      <p>Ask each candidate and the incumbent to show the same event trace, candidate preview, eligibility decision, inventory fallback, pause and order export. Mark unsupported requirements explicitly. A paired channel tool needs a demonstrated shared suppression path and combined cost estimate.</p><p>The launch record should include trigger definition, state transitions, offer approval, fixture outcomes, unresolved limitations, owner and rollback steps. Expand based on those checks and observed behavior. This page owns cart state; <a href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">browse abandonment</a> owns research intent, and the analytics page owns report integrity.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
